Right. The missing category is context labor.

Platforms already understand paying for visible outputs: videos, articles, designs, code, ads. But a lot of AI value comes from less visible work: correcting bad assumptions, supplying edge cases, explaining tacit knowledge, identifying useful abstractions, ranking alternatives, creating examples, exposing failure modes, and giving the system the context that lets it reason well.

That is not merely “content.” It is closer to information infrastructure.

A healthier market could distinguish among creators of:

* content — the artifact itself,
* data — observations, examples, labels, measurements,
* context — why something matters, how pieces relate, what is missing,
* judgment — ranking, criticism, taste, calibration,
* structure — taxonomies, schemas, workflows, ontologies,
* discovery — genuinely new patterns, methods, or connections.

And those have different economics. Ten thousand ordinary examples might be worth less than one correction that exposes a systematic failure mode.

So instead of paying purely for volume:

\text{value} \neq \text{tokens contributed}

you’d want something more like:

V = f(\text{novelty},\text{accuracy},\text{information gain},
\text{transferability},\text{downstream impact})

That creates the win-win you’re pointing at. The model/operator gets better inputs and stronger human participation; the person supplying unusually valuable cognition gets compensated rather than having that contribution disappear into an undifferentiated interaction stream.

The hard part is attribution. If one person’s insight gets transformed, combined with fifty others, and then improves millions of outputs, you need provenance and contribution accounting without pretending every causal effect can be perfectly measured.

That suggests a whole stack:

provenance → attribution → valuation → permission → compensation.

And potentially different payment forms: flat bounties, retainers, reputation-weighted consulting fees, usage royalties, prizes for discovered failure modes, or pools shared among contributors whose work materially improved a system.

The larger conceptual shift is simple:

People don’t only create things. They create better states of understanding.

If an economy can measure and purchase that reliably, “data analyst,” “critic,” “teacher,” “user,” “research participant,” and “consultant” start bleeding into a new occupation: context creator or cognitive supplier.

That may end up being a much larger market than conventional content creation.